birch.f - the source code
birch.fitdat - an example input file

Code description:
The code computes crystal structure minima: lattice parameter,
  bulk modulus, energy, etc., using the Birch-Murnaghan eqn.
The code does isotropic crystals (sc, Td, fcc, bc), in which
  the volume is a fixed factor times a0^3, or also by volume.
The units are assumed to be bohr and Rydberg.

Compilation:
It is a fully self-contained f77 code, no external dependencies.
Use g77 or f77 without any flags, and there is nothing to link.
The birch.f is an interactive, text-menu driven code.

Instructions to run the code:
Launch it interactively, and the code will ask you a
series of questions, which include:
(1) what file is the data in?
(2) where within that file is the data?
e.g. how many lines to skip in the data, where on each
line is the lattice parameter, energy.
(3) what is the crystal structure?
This is done to relate the input lattice parameter to
a volume, necessary to get the bulk modulus right.
The various options (sc, fcc, td, bcc) apply a scaling factor
appropriate to a^3 to get a volume (1.0 fr sc, 4.0 for fcc,
2.0 for bcc) and also allows you to input you own scaling
factor is you wish.
(4) What order of polynomial do you wish to use in the fit?
I have found "3" to be the best answer to this.  Using "2"
in a harmonic misses important anharmonicity, and larger than
three is usually looking for noise (and there is something
in the code which breaks on occasion with much larger poly's)

About the input file:
After skipping the number of lines indicated by the user,
the code reads data line-by-line, extracting the data
it needs from each line, stopping when it reaches an
error or EOF condition.  E.g.:
# C diamond 2at/16^3/k8^3/lda                                              
# a(bohr)     E(Rydberg)
   6.58    -22.8768965494
   6.60    -22.8777795944
   6.62    -22.8784558056
   6.63    -22.8787174113
x  6.64    -22.8789420664
   6.65    -22.8791061150
EOF
Here you would tell the code to skip the first two lines,
find the lattice parameter in position one, and energy in
position two, and it would read four valid data points,
ending when it tried to read a number and found a
character ("x") instead.
